# Removing a device — revocation and its two-part cascade.
# Authentication cascades automatically (any verifier that has seen the revoke
# rejects the whole subtree). Message-key access does NOT cascade for free: a
# revoked device physically still holds keys it was wrapped, so rotation must
# exclude the ENTIRE subtree.
direction: down

mr: "Member root\n(or an ancestor device)" { shape: hexagon; style.fill: "#ecfdf5" }
laptop: "Laptop" { style.fill: "#f1f5f9" }
phone: "Phone  ← revoke this" { style.fill: "#fee2e2"; style.stroke: "#b91c1c"; style.bold: true }
tablet: "Tablet\n(paired from phone)" { style.fill: "#fee2e2"; style.stroke: "#b91c1c" }

mr -> laptop: "device_delegation"
laptop -> phone: "device_delegation"
phone -> tablet: "device_delegation"

mr -> phone: "device_revoke\n{ device_pub=phone, revoker_pub=ancestor }" { style.stroke: "#b91c1c"; style.stroke-dash: 3 }

auth: |md
  **1 — Authentication cascade (free).** Every chain through the phone now fails
  at the revocation hop, so the **tablet dies too** without being named. Converges
  on every verifier, even one offline at the time (the log is an order-independent
  set; *revocation wins*). Revoked keys can never be re-paired.
| { style.font-size: 13 }

key: |md
  **2 — Message-key cascade (must be enacted).** The phone & tablet still hold the
  current room key. A remaining key-holder emits **room_key_rotate** to a new epoch,
  wrapped to the device keys of the **surviving** subtree only (`DeviceLog.Subtree`
  is excluded). Pre-revocation history stays readable — keys can't be un-shared.
| { style.font-size: 13 }

phone -> auth: "" { style.opacity: 0 }
tablet -> key: "" { style.opacity: 0 }
